ill 3.16 billion dollars it's almost  what they need to spend for to keep the  security or armed forces and police  which is 3.3 billion dollars  so it's it's a completely unreasonable  demand if mr isa and and the indigenous  leaders were they were that concerned  about the the welfare of of their  communities what they would ask is an  end of fuel subsidies which benefit the  urban elites and they will redirect that  money to other issues such as education  such as security such as for example  better  better uh uh  subsidies for for uh agricultural  products for for for agricultural inputs  okay so it's to me it's it's very clear  what's happening here okay  sorry if you're looking i've got to jump  in i want to get danny in here because  obviously danny you know we can talk  about oil we can talk about the economy  but we also need to talk about the role  literally of the indigenous community  from from your perspective because they  have since 1964 been able to assert  themselves in a way that very few  communities and minority groups that  other countries around the world have  been able to as a collective group  negotiate make the government sit and  talk to them that has taken time  where do you think the negotiations are  right now in terms of the way they're  talking to each other  leonidas isa and the konai said last  night they'll be back in the streets  today they've continued to call for  peaceful civil disobedience across the  the country  um overwhelmingly the repression has  come from the government side  we've seen five protesters killed uh so  far  we've seen  how many hundreds and hundreds of  protesters uh openly uh wounded uh  injured uh  imprisoned  so  there's an informational war right now  going on in ecuador and throughout the  world really they're trying to pin all  the violence on the protest movement  trying to give carte blanche to the  state security forces to continue to  carry out this repression and i think we  have to keep coming 